police in Turkey suspect a Syrian woman  planted the bomb that went off in  Istanbul on Sunday they say she was  trained by Kurdish militants the woman  is one of more than 40 people who have  been detained over the attack the band  Kurdistan Workers Party or pkk has  denied any role in the blast at least  six people were killed and several dozen  in injured when the explosion ripped  through a crowded pedestrian street in  near tuxim Square  police raid a home in Istanbul following  Sunday's Bomb Blast  inside a suspect is arrested  turkey's interior Ministry claims this  person said to be a Syrian woman with  links to Kurdish militants was  responsible for planting the device  dozens more people are sent to have been  detained  the attack hit this busy shopping street  causing panic across the city as people  ran for their lives  I was talking on the phone all of a  sudden there was an explosion it was  pretty loud I looked out the window and  saw people running it's very sad because  some people just came here to uh to have  fun to enjoy to have a vacation they  went to the shop to buy something and  they didn't know that they will be it  will be their last day  I don't feel safe here anymore I'm  afraid it will be more bombings if I  ever have kids I don't want them to go  through something like this  I hope it will not happen again and they  managed to prevent such attacks  condolences have been paid from across  the world  but turkey has rejected those from the  United States it accuses Washington of  supporting the band Kurdistan Workers  Party or pkk which it claims is behind  the attack  the insincerity of our so-called allies  who officially sent the terrorists money  from their own senates  is evident  head on that  while the political fights continue  Istanbul cleans up the mess as the most  deadly attack in five years leaves this  city on edge  our correspondent Julia Hahn is in  Istanbul and earlier she told me more  about the police investigation  well Turkish police have surprisingly  quickly identified and detained a main  suspect less than 24 hours after the  deadly attack a Syrian woman is believed  to have planted the bomb on istikal  Avenue here in central Istanbul  according to the police she received  training by the Kurdistan Workers Party  or pkk and its Syrian Affiliates and  entered turkey from northern Syria 46  other people were also detained in  connection with the attack police say  they went through the footage of more  than a thousand security cameras here in  Istanbul in order to be able to make  these investigations and arrests now the  Turkish government blames the Kurdish  militant pkk which is listed as a  terrorist organization by Ankara but  also by the European Union the Us and  other Western countries interestingly  the pkk itself published a statement  denying any involvement in this attack  so did another Kurdish let group The SDF  in Syria now that certainly raises some  questions but the means for us  journalists here in Turkey for anyone  basically to independently verify the  various claims coming from different  sides is uh very limited very difficult  here the government strictly controls  the flow of information yesterday  immediately after the blast they imposed  a new span to what they say in order to  uh avoid Panic spreading among the  population today they're widely sharing  the images of the main suspect and her  arrest but right now this is the main  source of information we have uh talk us  briefly through the reaction to this  most serious attack in five years  well there is of course a great sense of  sadness among Istanbul residents I've  seen many people uh paying a visit to  the site of the blast today laying down  flowers I've seen people crying and then  of course there is the realization among  many of us living here in Istanbul that  this blast could have hit any of us just  because istikal Avenue is such a central  place it's very popular with locals and  tourists alike and many people were  there on a Sunday when shops and  restaurants were open to enjoy their  free time and then of course this  explosion this blast brings back very  painful memories for people in Turkey of  a series of bombings and explosions  between the years of 2015 to 2017 which  Nationwide killed several hundred people  some of the attacks were blamed on the  so-called Islamic State group others  were blamed on Kurdish militants and and  that is what many people told me today  that they are afraid something like this  might be happening again  Juliana corresponded in Istanbul there  thank you very much Julian
 

